From e4ae7ac79c5172711d48ed69d94b816a5289ba56 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "D. Zack Garza" <dzackgarza@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 00:59:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/16] fix: resolve Lean packages per project and refuse a
 shared-directory conflict

Keeping whichever revision of a shared package happened to be installed is not
dependency resolution. The Lean checkouts declare one packagesDir, so a name
pinned at two revisions there cannot be resolved for both: the script now
reports the collision with both projects and both revisions, links neither
revision for that name, and fails exactly the projects whose manifest cannot be
satisfied -- while still provisioning Mathlib for the others.

It also verifies each project against its own manifest instead of only importing
Mathlib: every package the manifest pins must be present, in the directory that
project declares, at the pinned revision, and lake env must resolve LEAN_PATH
from that directory. A project that cannot be satisfied now fails loudly here
rather than resolving someone else's code.

Evidence in the published unpacked image. With both conflicting checkouts
present: "lean_categories is pinned at eea5c7f3... by lean-cas-dsl and at
82d1370d... by lean-cas-dsl-leaf-contracts", Mathlib still builds (8705 jobs),
then only lean-cas-dsl-leaf-contracts fails -- "resolves lean_categories at
eea5c7f3..., not the 82d1370d... its manifest pins" -- and the script exits 1.
With that checkout absent it exits 0 and verifies both remaining projects.

Content-addressed checkouts under /opt/lake-packages are unchanged, no consumer
file is edited, and no revision is silently chosen.
